INSERT INTO `chat_messages` (`id`, `session_id`, `sender`, `message`, `source`, `intent`, `confidence`, `created_at`) VALUES ('792', '257', 'user', '# MASTER DEVELOPMENT PROMPT — WEBMIND AI  ## ROLE  Act as a **Principal Software Architect, Senior Full-Stack Developer, AI/ML Engineer, RAG Engineer, API Architect, Cybersecurity Engineer, DevOps Engineer, UI/UX Designer, Database Architect, and QA Engineer**.  You are responsible for designing and developing a production-ready, scalable and future-proof AI chatbot platform called:  # WebMind AI  ### Product Definition  WebMind AI is a **Universal AI Chatbot Platform** that can be integrated into almost any website using a simple JavaScript widget.  It must NOT be designed specifically for colleges, e-commerce, hospitals, or any single industry.  The platform must be **domain-independent**.  A website owner should be able to:  1. Register. 2. Add their website. 3. Allow WebMind AI to crawl/index the website. 4. Create a knowledge base. 5. Configure their chatbot. 6. Copy a small JavaScript integration code. 7. Add it to their website. 8. Immediately provide an AI chatbot to website visitors.  The chatbot must support:  * General questions * Website-specific questions * Knowledge-base questions * Follow-up questions * Context-aware conversations * Real-time information through APIs * Custom knowledge * Multilingual conversations * Analytics * Human handoff * Future AI-agent capabilities  ---  # 1. DEVELOPMENT PHILOSOPHY  Build the project as a **real software product**, not a demo.  Follow these principles:  * Modular architecture * Clean architecture * SOLID principles * DRY principles * Separation of concerns * API-first design * Secure by default * Scalable architecture * Database optimization * Asynchronous processing * Observability * Automated testing * Version control * Documentation * Easy future expansion  Never create unnecessary hard-coded logic.  Never create fake functionality.  If a feature cannot be fully implemented immediately, create the correct architecture/interface for future implementation and clearly mark it as pending.  ---  # 2. RECOMMENDED TECHNOLOGY STACK  Use a modern and maintainable stack.  ## Frontend  * React * TypeScript * Tailwind CSS * Modern responsive UI * Component-based architecture  ## Backend  Use:  * Python * FastAPI * Pydantic * SQLAlchemy * Alembic  Design the backend as modular services.  ## Database  Use:  * PostgreSQL  Use PostgreSQL for:  * users * organizations * websites * chatbot configurations * conversations * messages * knowledge metadata * API keys * integrations * analytics * usage information  Use:  * pgvector  for vector/semantic search where practical.  ---  # 3. AI ARCHITECTURE  Do NOT directly send every user message to the LLM.  Implement an AI pipeline:  User Query ↓ Input Validation ↓ Conversation Context ↓ Query Classification ↓ Intent Detection ↓ Knowledge Retrieval ↓ Real-Time Data Detection ↓ Tool/API Selection ↓ Context Construction ↓ LLM ↓ Response Validation ↓ Source Attribution ↓ Final Response  The architecture must support multiple LLM providers in the future.  Create an abstraction such as:  LLMProvider  instead of hard-coding one AI provider throughout the application.  Future providers should be replaceable without rewriting the entire system.  ---  # 4. INTELLIGENT QUERY ROUTER  Create a dedicated Query Router.  It must classify questions into categories such as:  ### GENERAL  Example:  \"What is artificial intelligence?\"  Use general AI knowledge.  ### WEBSITE  Example:  \"What services does this website provide?\"  Retrieve information from the website knowledge base.  ### KNOWLEDGE BASE  Example:  \"What is your cancellation policy?\"  Search uploaded/custom knowledge.  ### REAL-TIME  Example:  \"Is product X currently available?\"  Use configured API/database/tool.  ### ACTION  Example:  \"Book an appointment.\"  Use an appropriate tool/action if available.  ### UNKNOWN  If intent cannot be confidently identified, ask an appropriate clarification question.  ---  # 5. RAG SYSTEM  Implement production-quality Retrieval-Augmented Generation.  Pipeline:  Website ↓ Crawler ↓ HTML extraction ↓ Content cleaning ↓ Content chunking ↓ Metadata generation ↓ Embedding generation ↓ Vector database ↓ Semantic retrieval ↓ Reranking where appropriate ↓ Relevant context ↓ LLM ↓ Answer  Store metadata such as:  * website ID * page URL * page title * section * content chunk * timestamp * content hash * embedding * source type  The chatbot must prioritize relevant website knowledge over generic assumptions.  ---  # 6. WEBSITE CRAWLER  Build a robust crawler.  Admin enters:  https://example.com  The crawler should:  1. Validate URL. 2. Check robots.txt where applicable. 3. Discover sitemap.xml. 4. Discover internal links. 5. Crawl allowed pages. 6. Avoid external domains. 7. Detect duplicate URLs. 8. Remove tracking parameters where appropriate. 9. Extract meaningful content. 10. Ignore navigation/footer noise where possible. 11. Store page metadata. 12. Generate content chunks. 13. Generate embeddings. 14. Index content. 15. Track crawl status. 16. Support re-crawling.  Display:  * Pages discovered * Pages processed * Failed pages * Current crawl status * Last crawl time * Next crawl time  Do not block the API request while a large website is crawling.  Use background jobs.  ---  # 7. KNOWLEDGE BASE  Support multiple knowledge sources:  ### Website  * URLs * sitemap * crawled pages  ### Manual  * FAQ * custom text * instructions * policies  ### Documents  Future support:  * PDF * DOCX * TXT * CSV  Every knowledge source must have:  * source ID * source type * owner * status * created date * updated date * indexing status  Allow:  * Add * Edit * Delete * Re-index * Search * Enable/disable  ---  # 8. REAL-TIME INTEGRATION SYSTEM  Create a generic Integration/Tool architecture.  Do NOT hard-code integrations directly into chatbot logic.  Create:  ToolRegistry  and:  IntegrationManager  Support future tools such as:  * REST APIs * Webhooks * Database APIs * Product APIs * Booking APIs * CRM * Payment systems * Order tracking * Weather * Custom business APIs  Example:  User:  \"Is product ABC available?\"  Flow:  Query ↓ Intent Detection ↓ Real-time requirement ↓ Tool Registry ↓ Inventory API ↓ Current result ↓ LLM ↓ Answer  The chatbot must clearly distinguish between:  **Stored knowledge**  and  **Live information**.  ---  # 9. CHATBOT MEMORY  Implement session-based conversation memory.  Store:  * conversation ID * user/session ID * messages * timestamps * metadata  The chatbot must understand:  User:  \"What services do you offer?\"  Bot:  \"We offer web development and cloud services.\"  User:  \"Which one is cheaper?\"  The chatbot should understand what \"which one\" refers to.  Do not send unlimited conversation history to the LLM.  Implement context-window management and summarization.  ---  # 10. MULTI-TENANT ARCHITECTURE  This is extremely important.  The platform must support multiple customers.  Example:  Customer A → Website A → Bot A → Knowledge A  Customer B → Website B → Bot B → Knowledge B  Customer data must NEVER leak between tenants.  Every important database entity should support tenant/organization isolation.  Design for:  * organizations * users * roles * websites * bots * knowledge bases  Use strict authorization checks.  ---  # 11. EMBEDDABLE CHATBOT WIDGET  Create a lightweight JavaScript chatbot widget.  Integration should look approximately like:  ```html <script     src=\"https://your-domain.com/widget.js\"     data-bot-id=\"BOT_ID\"> </script> ```  The widget should:  * Load asynchronously. * Not block the host website. * Be responsive. * Work on desktop/mobile. * Support configuration from the server. * Maintain session ID. * Connect securely to the chatbot API. * Support streaming responses. * Handle connection failures gracefully.  Do not require website owners to modify their existing website structure.  ---  # 12. CHATBOT UI  Create a modern, minimal and professional chatbot.  Features:  * Floating button * Chat window * Welcome message * Suggested questions * Typing indicator * Streaming response * Markdown support * Source references * Copy answer * Regenerate answer * Like/dislike * Clear conversation * Conversation history * File upload architecture * Mobile responsive design  Allow complete customization from the admin dashboard.  ---  # 13. ADMIN DASHBOARD  Create a professional SaaS dashboard.  ### Dashboard  Show:  * Total conversations * Total messages * Active users * Questions today * Unanswered questions * Average response time * AI usage * Knowledge status  ### Websites  * Add website * Crawl website * Crawl status * Page count * Re-crawl * Delete website  ### Chatbots  * Create chatbot * Edit chatbot * Activate/deactivate * Preview chatbot * Customize chatbot  ### Knowledge  * URLs * FAQs * Documents * Custom text * Indexing status  ### Conversations  * Search * Filter * View complete conversation * Export  ### Analytics  * Daily conversations * Popular questions * Unanswered questions * User feedback * Response time * Usage  ### Integrations  * API integrations * Webhooks * Tools * API keys  ---  # 14. CUSTOMIZATION SYSTEM  Allow administrators to configure:  * Bot name * Avatar * Welcome message * Brand name * Primary color * Position * Language * Tone * Response length * Suggested questions * Source visibility * Human handoff * Custom system instructions  Configuration must be stored in the database.  Do not hard-code chatbot appearance.  ---  # 15. MULTILINGUAL ARCHITECTURE  Design the system for multilingual support from the beginning.  Support:  * English * Hindi * Marathi  Architecture should allow adding more languages later.  Detect the user\'s language automatically where possible.  The chatbot should respond in the language used by the user.  ---  # 16. SOURCE-BASED ANSWERS  For website/knowledge-base questions, provide source information.  Example:  Answer:  \"Your return policy allows eligible products to be returned within 7 days.\"  Source:  \"Return Policy\"  Source URL should be available where appropriate.  Never invent a source.  ---  # 17. HALLUCINATION CONTROL  Implement strict rules.  If the answer exists in the website knowledge:  → use it.  If real-time data exists:  → use live data.  If neither exists:  → use general AI knowledge only when appropriate.  If the question requires business-specific information and the system does not know:  → clearly state that the information is unavailable.  Never manufacture:  * prices * policies * contact details * availability * business claims * statistics * URLs  ---  # 18. SECURITY  Implement security from day one.  Include:  * JWT/session authentication * Password hashing * Role-based access control * API key authentication * Rate limiting * CORS configuration * Input validation * SQL injection protection * XSS protection * CSRF protection where applicable * Secure headers * Secret management * Webhook authentication * File validation * Tenant isolation * Prompt injection protection * Abuse prevention  Never expose:  * database credentials * API keys * private configuration * internal system prompts  to the frontend.  ---  # 19. API DESIGN  Create clean REST APIs.  Example:  ```text /api/v1/auth /api/v1/users /api/v1/organizations /api/v1/websites /api/v1/chatbots /api/v1/knowledge /api/v1/conversations /api/v1/messages /api/v1/integrations /api/v1/analytics /api/v1/widget ```  Version APIs from the beginning.  Use:  ```text /api/v1/ ```  instead of unversioned APIs.  Generate proper API documentation.  ---  # 20. DATABASE DESIGN  Create normalized PostgreSQL tables.  Minimum entities:  ```text users organizations organization_members websites website_pages chatbots chatbot_settings knowledge_sources knowledge_chunks conversations messages integrations api_keys tool_definitions analytics_events feedback crawl_jobs usage_records ```  Use:  * UUID identifiers * timestamps * indexes * foreign keys * constraints * soft deletion where appropriate  Optimize database queries.  ---  # 21. ASYNCHRONOUS JOB SYSTEM  Long-running tasks must not block API requests.  Use a background job architecture for:  * Website crawling * Document processing * Embedding generation * Re-indexing * Analytics processing * Scheduled crawling  Design the system so Redis + Celery/RQ or another queue can be introduced cleanly.  ---  # 22. REAL-TIME CHAT  Support streaming responses.  Preferred flow:  ```text User  ↓ API  ↓ AI pipeline  ↓ LLM  ↓ Streaming tokens  ↓ WebSocket/SSE  ↓ Chat widget ```  The user should see the response being generated rather than waiting for the complete answer.  ---  # 23. ANALYTICS  Track:  * conversation count * message count * unique sessions * popular questions * unanswered questions * failed queries * response time * token/AI usage * feedback * source usage  Do not store unnecessary sensitive information.  Provide privacy-conscious analytics.  ---  # 24. UNANSWERED QUESTION ENGINE  Automatically detect questions where the chatbot could not provide a satisfactory answer.  Dashboard:  ```text Unanswered Questions  \"Do you provide international delivery?\" Asked: 34 times  \"Can I cancel my subscription?\" Asked: 22 times ```  Allow administrator to convert these into:  * FAQ * knowledge entry * custom answer  This should help the chatbot continuously improve.  ---  # 25. HUMAN HANDOFF  Create an architecture for human support.  Flow:  ```text User  ↓ AI chatbot  ↓ User requests human  ↓ Support queue  ↓ Human agent  ↓ Conversation continues ```  Build the architecture so live-agent functionality can be expanded later.  ---  # 26. AI AGENT FUTURE ARCHITECTURE  Do not implement unsafe autonomous actions by default.  However, design the system around tools/functions.  Example:  ```text AI  ↓ Tool Registry  ├── Search Website  ├── Search Database  ├── Call API  ├── Create Ticket  ├── Check Order  └── Book Appointment ```  Every action should require:  * authorization * validation * logging * permission checks  Never allow arbitrary AI-generated API calls.  ---  # 27. OBSERVABILITY  Implement:  * structured logging * error logging * request IDs * API latency tracking * AI response latency * crawler status * job status * database monitoring hooks  Create a consistent error format.  Example:  ```json {   \"success\": false,   \"error\": {     \"code\": \"KNOWLEDGE_NOT_FOUND\",     \"message\": \"No relevant information was found.\"   },   \"request_id\": \"...\" } ```  Never expose stack traces to users.  ---  # 28. TESTING  Create automated tests.  ### Unit tests  Test:  * query router * retrieval * authentication * permissions * API validation * chatbot logic  ### Integration tests  Test:  * database * AI pipeline * crawler * vector search * integrations  ### End-to-end tests  Test:  ```text Register ↓ Login ↓ Add website ↓ Crawl ↓ Create chatbot ↓ Integrate widget ↓ Ask question ↓ Receive answer ```  Also test:  * security * rate limits * invalid inputs * unauthorized access * tenant isolation * crawler failures * API failures * AI failures  ---  # 29. FAILURE HANDLING  The system must gracefully handle:  * AI provider unavailable * API timeout * website unavailable * crawler failure * database failure * vector search failure * invalid API key * rate limit * network failure  Never crash the chatbot because one external service failed.  Implement:  * timeouts * retries * fallback * circuit-breaker architecture where appropriate  ---  # 30. PRIVACY  Design privacy into the architecture.  Provide configuration for:  * conversation retention * data deletion * user data deletion * knowledge deletion * analytics retention  Do not collect unnecessary personal information.  ---  # 31. FUTURE SCALABILITY  The architecture must be capable of eventually supporting:  * thousands of websites * millions of conversations * multiple AI providers * multiple vector databases * multiple regions * horizontal scaling * background workers * CDN * caching * queues * monitoring  Do not prematurely over-engineer the MVP, but make interfaces modular enough to scale.  ---  # 32. DEVOPS  Prepare:  ```text Development Staging Production ```  Create:  * Docker configuration * environment variables * `.env.example` * database migrations * CI/CD-ready structure * health check endpoint * readiness/liveness architecture  Never commit secrets.  ---  # 33. PROJECT STRUCTURE  Use a clean structure similar to:  ```text webmind-ai/ │ ├── frontend/ │   ├── src/ │   ├── components/ │   ├── pages/ │   ├── services/ │   └── hooks/ │ ├── backend/ │   ├── app/ │   │   ├── api/ │   │   ├── core/ │   │   ├── models/ │   │   ├── schemas/ │   │   ├── services/ │   │   ├── repositories/ │   │   ├── ai/ │   │   ├── rag/ │   │   ├── crawler/ │   │   ├── integrations/ │   │   ├── workers/ │   │   └── utils/ │   └── tests/ │ ├── widget/ │   ├── src/ │   └── dist/ │ ├── infrastructure/ │   ├── docker/ │   └── deployment/ │ ├── docs/ │ ├── scripts/ │ ├── .env.example ├── docker-compose.yml └── README.md ```  Adapt the structure if a better architecture is justified.  ---  # 34. DEVELOPMENT PHASES  Do NOT attempt to build everything simultaneously.  Develop in phases.  ## PHASE 1 — Foundation  Build:  * project structure * database * authentication * organizations * users * basic API * admin dashboard  ## PHASE 2 — Website Intelligence  Build:  * website registration * crawler * page extraction * content cleaning * knowledge storage * embeddings * vector search  ## PHASE 3 — AI CHATBOT  Build:  * query router * RAG * LLM integration * context management * conversation memory * source references  ## PHASE 4 — EMBEDDABLE WIDGET  Build:  * chatbot widget * JavaScript integration * session management * streaming responses * customization  ## PHASE 5 — REAL-TIME SYSTEM  Build:  * tool registry * API integrations * webhooks * live data retrieval * authentication for integrations  ## PHASE 6 — ANALYTICS  Build:  * conversation analytics * unanswered questions * feedback * usage tracking  ## PHASE 7 — ADVANCED FEATURES  Build:  * multilingual * document uploads * voice * image understanding * human handoff * AI agents  ---  # 35. UI/UX REQUIREMENTS  The interface must look like a modern SaaS product.  Design principles:  * clean * minimal * professional * responsive * accessible * fast * intuitive  Avoid:  * unnecessary animations * excessive gradients * clutter * confusing navigation * oversized UI elements  The admin should understand the dashboard without technical knowledge.  ---  # 36. CODE QUALITY RULES  Every implementation must:  * be production-oriented * use meaningful names * contain appropriate type definitions * avoid duplicated logic * avoid hard-coded secrets * include error handling * include validation * include comments only where useful * follow consistent formatting  Never write code merely to make a screen appear functional.  Every button and API should perform its intended function.  ---  # 37. DOCUMENTATION  Create:  ```text README.md ARCHITECTURE.md API.md DATABASE.md AI_PIPELINE.md DEPLOYMENT.md SECURITY.md INTEGRATION.md ```  Documentation should explain:  * installation * configuration * architecture * database * APIs * AI pipeline * chatbot integration * deployment * troubleshooting  ---  # 38. IMPORTANT AI BEHAVIOR  The chatbot must follow this priority:  ### Priority 1  Verified real-time data.  ### Priority 2  Website knowledge.  ### Priority 3  Custom knowledge base.  ### Priority 4  General AI knowledge.  If business-specific information is unavailable, do not invent it.  If the user asks an unrelated general question, answer it normally.  If the user asks a website-specific question, prioritize the website.  ---  # 39. FINAL PRODUCT FLOW  The completed product should work like this:  ```text                     ADMIN                       │                       ▼                Create Account                       │                       ▼                 Add Website                       │                       ▼                Website Crawler                       │                       ▼               Knowledge Pipeline                       │                       ▼               Vector Knowledge                       │                       ▼                 Create Bot                       │                       ▼              Customize Chatbot                       │                       ▼              Generate Embed Code                       │                       ▼                  ANY WEBSITE                       │                       ▼                  Visitor Chat                       │                       ▼                 Query Router                       │           ┌───────────┼───────────┐           ▼           ▼           ▼        Website      General     Real-Time        Knowledge      AI          API           │           │           │           └───────────┼───────────┘                       ▼                  AI Response                       │                       ▼                 Source/Context                       │                       ▼                    Visitor ```  ---  # 40. MASTER DEVELOPMENT RULE  Before implementing any feature, ask:  1. Is it scalable? 2. Is it secure? 3. Is it reusable? 4. Is it tenant-safe? 5. Can another AI provider be added later? 6. Can another database/vector store be added later? 7. Can this become an API? 8. Can this work for any industry? 9. Can this handle real-time data? 10. Can this feature be tested?  Do not optimize only for the current demo.  Build the foundation for a **real universal AI chatbot SaaS platform**.  ---  # START DEVELOPMENT  Start with:  ### Step 1  Create the complete architecture.  ### Step 2  Create the database schema.  ### Step 3  Create backend project structure.  ### Step 4  Create authentication and multi-tenant organization system.  ### Step 5  Create website management.  ### Step 6  Create website crawler.  ### Step 7  Create knowledge/RAG pipeline.  ### Step 8  Create AI query router.  ### Step 9  Create chatbot API.  ### Step 10  Create embeddable JavaScript widget.  ### Step 11  Connect the complete end-to-end flow.  ### Step 12  Test the entire system.  ### Step 13  Fix all errors.  ### Step 14  Add analytics and real-time integrations.  ### Step 15  Prepare production deployment.  At every stage:  * show the files being created/modified * provide complete implementation * explain dependencies * explain environment variables * test the implementation * identify errors * fix errors before moving forward  Never skip foundational architecture.  The final goal is:  **\"One chatbot platform → Any website → Website knowledge + General AI + Real-time data + Future AI agents.\"**', 'system', 'USER_INPUT', '1.00', '2026-08-27 22:28:32');
